Chapter: GroundSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Ground
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Ground: a quad on the floor, and the three ways it can come to nothing.

 Ground quads never enter the depth sort -- they are painted first, in walk
 order, and everything that stands up is painted over them -- so this is a
 pipeline of its own and nothing in judge/ grades it as one.

 THE EIGHT-POINT GUARD IS THE ZIG'S FIXED BUFFER AND IT REFUSES RATHER THAN
 TRUNCATES. A caller handing in nine points gets NOTHING, not the first eight,
 and the chapter says why: a silently shortened ground quad would be a wrong
 shape painted confidently. That is a decision, not an implementation detail,
 and it is the kind that gets "simplified" by someone who reads the buffer size
 as a limit rather than as a contract. Both sides of it are graded -- eight
 points draw, nine draw nothing.

 THE OTHER TWO WAYS TO COME TO NOTHING are the near plane and the polygon guard,
 and they are different failures with the same result. A quad entirely behind
 the eye is clipped to fewer than three vertices and dropped; a caller handing
 in two points is dropped before any clipping happens. Grading both is what
 keeps a rewrite from collapsing them into one test in the wrong place.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the drop is d^2 / 2R with R = 100000,
 which is exact arithmetic and graded at 0.0; the command count and its tag come
 from `push-poly`, which emits exactly one tag-0 command or none. The projected
 COORDINATES are not graded here -- they are Camera's, and CameraCheck grades
 them -- so this asserts the count of them and not their values, which is the
 honest half of what this chapter is responsible for.

 We say:

Section: The Curvature Drop

 A ground vertex keeps its point and hangs its height below the tangent plane.
 The sign is the assertion: the height is NEGATIVE, because the ground falls
 away, and a missing negation would bow the road upward into the sky.

  pts-a : List RiderPt
  pts-a =
    [ RiderPt { right = 0.0, forward = 0.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 3.0, forward = 10.0 },
      RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = 40.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 12.5, forward = 100.0 } ]

  height-got : List Real
  height-got = list-map (\v -> v.height) (ground-verts pts-a 0)

  height-want : List Real
  height-want = [0.0, -0.000545, -0.008125, -0.05078125]

 The point itself is carried through untouched.

  carry-got : List Real
  carry-got =
    let vs = ground-verts pts-a 0
    in [(list-at vs 1).right, (list-at vs 1).forward, (list-at vs 3).right, (list-at vs 3).forward]

  carry-want : List Real
  carry-want = [3.0, 10.0, 12.5, 100.0]

Section: What Comes Out

 A square ten metres on a side, well in front of the eye. One command, tag 0,
 carrying the colour it was given and two coordinates per vertex.

  quad : List RiderPt
  quad =
    [ RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = 10.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 5.0, forward = 10.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 5.0, forward = 20.0 },
      RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = 20.0 } ]

  behind : List RiderPt
  behind =
    [ RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = -10.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 5.0, forward = -10.0 },
      RiderPt { right = 5.0, forward = -20.0 },
      RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = -20.0 } ]

  two-points : List RiderPt
  two-points = [RiderPt { right = -5.0, forward = 10.0 }, RiderPt { right = 5.0, forward = 10.0 }]

  wide : Integer -> List RiderPt
  wide (n) =
    if n <= 0 then []
    else [RiderPt { right = real-from-int n, forward = 10.0 + real-from-int n }] & wide (n - 1)

  drawn : List RiderPt -> List DrawCmd
  drawn (ps) = emit-ground-color ps 3112588 685.5110432362151 960.0

  count-got : List Integer
  count-got =
    [ list-length (drawn quad),
      list-length (drawn (wide 8)),
      list-length (drawn (wide 9)),
      list-length (drawn behind),
      list-length (drawn two-points) ]

  count-want : List Integer
  count-want = [1, 1, 0, 0, 0]

 The one command a good quad produces: tag 0 is a flat polygon, the colour is
 the one handed in, and `pts` is flattened, so four vertices are eight reals.

  cmd-got : List Integer
  cmd-got =
    let c = list-at (drawn quad) 0
    in [c.tag, c.color, c.color2, list-length c.pts, list-length c.geom]

  cmd-want : List Integer
  cmd-want = [0, 3112588, 0, 8, 0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gr-height" height-got height-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gr-carry " carry-got carry-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gr-count " count-got count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gr-cmd   " cmd-got cmd-want)
  end
